#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161672 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebcdc0f-dc9b-54ec-9cc5-49b2c046e8b7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance of a Sample Mean"}] :question/id #uuid "486f4292-2f50-5d89-be1a-b3c054b449e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-10082" :question/problem "A sample $X_1, X_2$ of two pills is drawn from a population. The population has pills of two different active ingredient concentrations, $30\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}$ and $\\textrm{15}\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}$, distributed in the ratio $1:2$. Calculate $\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]$." :question/worked-solution "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the pills in the population have concentrations of $30\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}$ and $15\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}$, and these are distributed in the ratio $1:2$. Therefore, to compute the population mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of\n\n$$\n1+2 = 3\n$$\n\npills with only the following concentrations:\n\n$$\n30\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}, \\qquad 15\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}, \\qquad 15\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nμ &= \\frac{1}{3}∑_(i)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(30 + 2 \\cdot 15) \\\\\n&= \\frac{60}{3} \\\\\n&= 20mg/ml\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ^{2} &= \\frac{1}{3}∑_(i)x_{i}^{2} - μ^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(30^{2} + 2 \\cdot 15^{2}) - 20^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(900 + 450) - 400 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 1350 - 400 \\\\\n&= 450 - 400 \\\\\n&= 50(mg/ml)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the sample size $n=2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] =\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n} = \\dfrac{50}{2} = 25\\,(\\textrm{mg/ml})^2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161673 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162107 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6eac4a2e-cfa8-5476-919e-fd3c26d01d75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Error"}] :question/id #uuid "41713dda-2195-5bd5-b0be-941b8188cb25" :question/math-academy-id "e-10083" :question/problem "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{200}$ is a sample of size $n=200$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu = 23$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = 4$, calculate the standard error of $\\overline{X}$. Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}\n$$\n\nSince the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = 4\n$$\n\nand the sample size is $n=200$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X^(―)] &= \\frac{4}{200} \\\\\n&= 0.02\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{0.02} \\approx 0.14\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161674 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162108 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9453a0f1-029b-5436-94c1-47ee311f6e4d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Appropriate Sample Size"}] :question/id #uuid "0f59a118-79ce-5746-8917-6f4092dcbc6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10084" :question/problem "A sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ of $n$ coconuts is drawn from a population. The population has coconuts of two different weights, $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}$ and $2\\,\\textrm{lb}$, distributed in the ratio $2:3$. Determine the smallest sample size required to give a standard error of less than $0.02\\,\\textrm{lb}$." :question/worked-solution "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the coconuts in the population have weights $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}$ and $2\\,\\textrm{lb}$, and these are distributed in the ratio $2:3$. Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of\n\n$$\n2+3 = 5\n$$\n\ncoconuts with only the following weights:\n\n$$\n1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 2\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 2\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 2\\,\\textrm{lb}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nμ &= \\frac{1}{5}∑_(i)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}(2 \\cdot 1.5 + 3 \\cdot 2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}(3 + 6) \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{5} \\\\\n&= 1.8lb\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ^{2} &= \\frac{1}{5}∑_(i)x_{i}^{2} - μ^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}(2 \\cdot 1.5^{2} + 3 \\cdot 2^{2}) - 1.8^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}(16.5) - 3.24 \\\\\n&= 3.3 - 3.24 \\\\\n&= 0.06lb^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is\n\n$$\n\\sigma= \\sqrt{0.06}\n$$\n\nand we have that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.06}{n}}\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.02\\,\\textrm{lb}$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{\\frac{0.06}{n}} & < 0.02 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{0.06}}{\\sqrt{n}} & < 0.02 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & > \\frac{\\sqrt{0.06}}{0.02} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & > \\sqrt{\\frac{0.06}{0.02^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & > \\sqrt{\\frac{0.06}{0.0004}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & > \\sqrt{150} \\\\ n & > 150\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=151$."}]}
